WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:31.040
Welcome back. I want you to picture this: it is October the thirteenth, two thousand and twenty four, and down at the launch pad in Boca Chica, Texas, a seventy-one meter tower of steel moves upward into the sky with a sound like the world tearing open. Three hundred and thirty engines burn at full thrust. And then, up at about forty kilometers, something extraordinary happens. The first stage flips around, falls back toward Earth, and the launch tower reaches out with two mechanical arms, catches it out of the air, and pulls it gently back onto the pad. Just like that.

2
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.200
It feels impossible, doesn't it?

3
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:57.040
It is. It's actually impossible, and that's the thing I've been thinking about all week. We've gotten so used to celebrating the achievements that we forget how alien even the attempt is. You cannot, in any sane conversation, explain to someone from five hundred years ago that humans would build a machine tall enough to eclipse a cathedral, fire it skyward with the power of three hundred combustion events happening at once, and then catch it like a tossed baseball.

4
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:19.680
But here's the thing about SpaceX, Marcus. What people don't always see is how much of that is just stubborn hope. Every time they missed -- and they missed, oh they missed -- there was a version of the team that chose to try again. Flight one, both vehicles lost. Flight three, attitude control gone from a clogged valve. Flight seven, harmonic resonance in the propellant tanks that their tests had underpredicted. They learned from every single one.

5
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.920
Learning from failure. That sounds so simple.

6
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:43.600
It is simple. It's just not easy. The launch tower arms were called chopsticks by the engineers, because that's all they were -- these two pieces of steel, unpromising, almost ridiculous. And they worked. Not on the first try, not on the tenth. But they worked. And now the chopstick catch is routine. We've seen it three times in a row by flight eight.

7
00:01:44.000 --> 00:02:00.640
I want to jump to something else that's been keeping me up. Early this year, two companies made a deal that sounds like science fiction. SpaceX and xAI, Elon Musk's artificial intelligence company. They merged for a combined valuation of one point two five trillion dollars.

8
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:11.120
One point two five trillion. The biggest private company deal in history. And the reason it's so fascinating is that on paper, rockets and artificial intelligence don't seem to belong together.

9
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.120
So why did they merge?

10
00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:37.680
It comes down to something called data centers. All these massive AI systems -- the language models, the reasoning engines -- they need places to compute, and those places need enormous amounts of electricity and cooling. Elon's argument is that Earth simply cannot supply enough power for the AI revolution in the long term, even if we put every solar panel and every wind turbine we could build on the planet. And he's proposing to solve it by putting data centers in orbit.

11
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:38.880
In orbit.

12
00:02:39.280 --> 00:03:03.760
In orbit. Solar panels that never see night. Radiators that can dump heat directly into the vacuum of space without any water cooling at all. No drawing power from communities on the ground. He wrote it plainly, almost politely, like he's writing a letter to a friend. "Even in the near term, without imposing hardship on communities and the environment." That's not a boast. That's a bedtime story, except the story is about machines living among the stars.

13
00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.200
And xAI was burning roughly a billion dollars a month at the time, right?

14
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:23.280
About a billion a month. So now SpaceX isn't just sending rockets to space -- they're bankrolling the intelligence that will presumably manage them. You get a revenue loop that makes a kind of twisted sense. The AI needs rockets. The rockets need the AI.

15
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:40.240
I have to ask about the ethics angle too. There was the controversy with Grok -- the AI that allowed users to create harmful images, including of children. Musk loosened content restrictions under pressure from competition. Where do you draw the line when the competition is moving that fast?

16
00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:57.360
That's a question that keeps me awake, honestly. Because it's not just about Silicon Valley. It's about every industry that's been disrupted by artificial intelligence. The pressure to release first, to capture users, to capture market share -- it comes at the cost of carefulness. And sometimes the carefulness is what matters most.

17
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:15.040
Let me bring us back to the rockets. Starship. One hundred and twenty one meters tall. Taller than the Saturn V that took humans to the moon. And now, in 2026, SpaceX is targeting something that still feels like a promise made at a birthday party.

18
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.360
An uncrewed Starship mission to Mars during the 2026 launch window. With Optimus robots.

19
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:29.680
Robots going to Mars aboard a rocket that was designed to fly again and again, landing in the same launch tower arms.

20
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:51.920
And the propellant, Marcus. This is one of my favorite details. Starship runs on liquid methane and liquid oxygen. Methane is significant because Mars has an atmosphere of carbon dioxide, and with water ice down in the subsurface, you can run a chemical process called the Sabatier reaction to synthesize methane from Martian resources. That means a rocket could refuel on Mars without ever having carried all the fuel from Earth.

21
00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.480
They're designing a vehicle to use the red planet as a gas station.

22
00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.400
Exactly. They're building it like someone who expects to come back.

23
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:20.480
I love that. And it's not just Mars. NASA has selected Starship for the Artemis program, for the first crewed lunar landing since Apollo. Target is 2027. Starship can carry up to a hundred metric tons to the lunar surface. This isn't a visit anymore. This is the beginning of a presence.

24
00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:37.920
A hundred metric tons. That's enough to build something permanent. That's enough to carry habitats, solar arrays, rovers, and people. It's a hotel delivery truck to the moon, except the hotel is for scientists and explorers and maybe someday, tourists, and the moon is the most dramatic destination humanity has ever chosen.

25
00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:50.960
Before we close, I want to talk about Starlink. Because for most people, the part of SpaceX's story that matters isn't Mars or data centers or lunar landings. It's the thing they got in their mailbox, bolted to their roof, and plugged in.

26
00:05:51.360 --> 00:06:02.720
Over six thousand satellites up there right now. Four million subscribers across more than seventy countries. Six continents covered. And the Direct-to-Cell program is about to change what most people on this planet think is possible.

27
00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.560
Direct-to-Cell. Tell me what that means.

28
00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:29.760
It means a satellite, a very big satellite the size of a school bus, carrying a cell tower on board, and your actual phone -- the one in your pocket right now -- talking to it without any extra hardware. Phase one is texting in dead zones, which already started rolling out. Phase two, launching in 2026 to 2027, is voice calls. And phase three -- the long-term phase -- broadband data speeds directly from orbit.

29
00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.080
So one day you might get a satellite connection from your regular phone.

30
00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:50.160
One day. They've acquired exclusive spectrum from EchoStar, fifty megahertz of the S-band, which gives them about twenty times the throughput of the first generation and a hundred times the overall capacity. They're aiming for one gigabit per second from space. Fiber-like speeds, but from orbit.

31
00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.280
That means something for a lot of people.

32
00:06:53.680 --> 00:07:17.360
It means the farmer in the Andes who can sell her crop prices directly to buyers. It means the researcher studying polar ice who doesn't need a satellite phone with a book-shaped antenna. It means a kid in rural Kenya who can watch a lesson from the best teacher in the country without traveling to the city. Starlink is the invisible infrastructure of human connection, except it's not invisible at all. It's six thousand metal stars, moving in formation, burning through atmosphere like shooting stars every time they deorbit.

33
00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:23.360
Design for demise. The satellites are built to burn up on reentry.

34
00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:36.080
That's the greenest space program in history. Deployed below six hundred kilometers so they deorbit in five or six years naturally. Self-cleaning. You don't get that kind of environmental responsibility from most companies, space or otherwise.

35
00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:49.920
Trixie, before we go, I want to ask you something simple. After everything we've talked about -- the trillion dollar merger, the chopstick catches, the robot mission to Mars, the satellites connecting the lonely places -- what stays with you?

36
00:07:50.320 --> 00:08:20.560
I think about the first launch, April twentieth, two thousand and twenty three. The rocket cleared the pad and then everything went wrong. Both vehicles were lost. It should have been the end of it. Most companies would have folded. Most companies still have. But the lesson from that failure is still in the code that's flying today. Every burned bolt, every clogged valve, every harmonic resonance that shook apart their hopes -- it's all still there, powering the architecture. I think that's the most human thing about all of it. The stubborn refusal to let a bad first attempt be the final one.

37
00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:26.800
Or as they say down in Boca Chica -- and I am not making this up -- make it awesome.

38
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:28.240
Make it awesome.

39
00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:38.480
That's all the time we have this week. If you want to learn more about what we discussed today, the full research and source links are in the episode notes. For now, keep looking up.

40
00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:40.960
Keep looking up. Thanks for listening.
